\chapter{User Codecs, Abstract I/O and Static Binary
Streams}\label{ch33-user-codecs-abstract-io-and-static-binary-streams}

The user defines data syntax and backend operations. MuDSL provides
protocols, template forwarding, byte-stream helpers and static byte
matching.

\section{Serializer and deserializer
protocols}\label{ch33-serializer-and-deserializer-protocols}

\texttt{Serializer\textless{}\allowbreak{}Backend\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
/ \texttt{Serializer!Backe\allowbreak{}nd} borrows a backend with
\texttt{serialize(\allowbreak{}value,\allowbreak{} output)\allowbreak{}}.
\texttt{Deserializer} borrows one with
\texttt{deserialize\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}input)\allowbreak{}}
in C++ or \texttt{deserialize!T(\allowbreak{}input)\allowbreak{}} in D.
Input/output types and operation results are chosen by the backend; they
need not be I/O adapters or binary streams.

The following user codec chooses a four-byte, big-endian integer syntax.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <cassert>

struct IntegerCodec {
    void serialize(const int& value, dsl::BinaryStream& output) {
        output.write_integer(static_cast<std::uint32_t>(value), dsl::ByteOrder::Big);
    }
    template<class T> T deserialize(dsl::BinaryStream& input) {
        return static_cast<T>(input.read_integer<std::uint32_t>(dsl::ByteOrder::Big));
    }
};

int main() {
    IntegerCodec codec;
    dsl::Serializer serializer(codec);
    dsl::Deserializer deserializer(codec);
    dsl::BinaryStream encoded;
    serializer.serialize(42, encoded);
    assert(deserializer.deserialize<int>(encoded) == 42);
}
\end{code}

\begin{code}
struct IntegerCodec
{
    void serialize(int value, ref BinaryStream output)
    { output.write_integer(cast(uint) value, ByteOrder.Big); }
    T deserialize(T)(ref BinaryStream input)
    { return cast(T) input.read_integer!uint(ByteOrder.Big); }
}

IntegerCodec codec;
auto serializer = Serializer!IntegerCodec(codec);
auto deserializer = Deserializer!IntegerCodec(codec);
BinaryStream encoded;
serializer.serialize(42, encoded);
assert(deserializer.deserialize!int(encoded) == 42);
\end{code}

C++ protocol queries are
\texttt{SerializerBacken\allowbreak{}d\textless{}\allowbreak{}B,\allowbreak{} T,\allowbreak{} Output\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{DeserializerBack\allowbreak{}end\textless{}\allowbreak{}B,\allowbreak{} T,\allowbreak{} Input\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
concepts. D uses the corresponding
\texttt{SerializerBacken\allowbreak{}d!(\allowbreak{}B,\allowbreak{} T,\allowbreak{} Output)\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{DeserializerBack\allowbreak{}end!(\allowbreak{}B,\allowbreak{} T,\allowbreak{} Input)\allowbreak{}}
templates. A backend can return its own result/error object; forwarding
preserves the return type. The adapter does not add a wire format or
schema.

\section{Abstract byte I/O}\label{ch33-abstract-byte-io}

