\texttt{IO} borrows its backend. In C++ it stores a reference; in D the
constructor takes a \texttt{ref} backend and stores a pointer. Keep the
backend alive and at a stable location for the adapter's use. The
backend owns its resource policy.

Here is a user-defined sequential memory backend in D:

\begin{code}
struct MemoryBackend
{
    ubyte[] data;
    size_t pos;
    size_t read(ubyte[] target)
    {
        auto available = data.length - pos;
        auto n = target.length < available ? target.length : available;
        target[0 .. n] = data[pos .. pos + n];
        pos += n;
        return n;
    }
    size_t write(const(ubyte)[] source)
    { data ~= source; return source.length; }
}

MemoryBackend backend;
auto io = IO!MemoryBackend(backend);
ubyte[] payload = [0, 255, 65];
io.write_all(payload);
auto stream = BinaryStream.load(io);
assert(stream.bytes == payload);
\end{code}

The C++ form substitutes a vector, spans and
\texttt{IO io(\allowbreak{}backend)\allowbreak{}}. A user can equally
supply a file, socket, device or callback backend with this protocol.

\subsection{Transfers and optional
operations}\label{ch33-transfers-and-optional-operations}

\texttt{read}/\texttt{write} validate that a backend's count does not
exceed its buffer. Short transfers are permitted. Zero read denotes EOF.
\texttt{read\_\allowbreak{}exact} fills an entire buffer or raises an
exception on premature EOF; \texttt{write\_\allowbreak{}all} completes
all output or raises an exception when a write makes no progress.
Backend exceptions propagate.

\texttt{seek(\allowbreak{}position)\allowbreak{}} and
\texttt{tell(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} are available when the backend
implements them. The core read/write protocol does not require random
access. The example above therefore has neither operation.

\section{Static byte regular
expressions}\label{ch33-static-byte-regular-expressions}

\texttt{StaticRegex\textless{}\allowbreak{}Source\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
in C++ and \texttt{StaticRegex!Sour\allowbreak{}ce} in D compile a
Thompson NFA from a string template argument. Invalid supported-syntax
patterns are diagnosed at compilation. Matching uses byte values, so
NUL, newlines and bytes from \texttt{0x80} through \texttt{0xff} are
ordinary input values.

