The current engine does not supply captures, counted repetitions,
backreferences or shorthand classes such as \texttt{\textbackslash{}d}.
Use \texttt{[0-9]} for digits.

\begin{code}
assert(dsl::StaticRegex<"^(ab|a)+[0-9]?$">::matches("aba7"));
std::vector<std::byte> bytes{std::byte{0}, std::byte{255}, std::byte{'A'}};
assert(dsl::StaticRegex<"\\x00[\\x80-\\xff]A">::matches(bytes));
\end{code}

\begin{code}
assert(StaticRegex!"^(ab|a)+[0-9]?$".matches("aba7"));
ubyte[] bytes = [0, 255, 65];
assert(StaticRegex!`\x00[\x80-\xff]A`.matches(bytes));
\end{code}

\texttt{matches} requires the entire input.
\texttt{prefix(\allowbreak{}bytes,\allowbreak{} start)\allowbreak{}}
returns an optional longest matching end offset.
\texttt{search(\allowbreak{}bytes,\allowbreak{} start)\allowbreak{}}
returns the earliest matching \texttt{RegexMatch}, choosing its longest
end at that start. Match records have \texttt{begin}, \texttt{end}, and
\texttt{size(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. C++ uses
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}; D uses
\texttt{Nullable}. Absolute \texttt{\textasciicircum{}}/\texttt{\$}
assertions still refer to the full input when \texttt{start} is nonzero.

The existing \texttt{pattern} and PEG \texttt{CompiledPattern} APIs have
their own smaller syntax and matching semantics. Static byte regexes
provide the grouping, alternation and binary-stream matching facility
documented here.

\section{Binary stream operations}\label{ch33-binary-stream-operations}

\texttt{BinaryStream} owns \texttt{bytes} and a read cursor
\texttt{pos}. C++ accepts an input span; D accepts a
\texttt{const(\allowbreak{}ubyte)\allowbreak{}[]}; each constructor
copies the input. \texttt{read(\allowbreak{}count)\allowbreak{}} returns
a const view and advances the cursor, raising an exception on EOF.
\texttt{write(\allowbreak{}input)\allowbreak{}} appends to storage
rather than writing at the cursor.

\begin{code}
dsl::BinaryStream stream(bytes);
auto token = stream.next<"\\0[\\x80-\\xff]">();
assert(token && token->size() == 2);
assert(stream.pos == 2);
\end{code}

\begin{code}
auto stream = BinaryStream(bytes);
auto token = stream.next!`\0[\x80-\xff]`();
assert(token.get.length == 2 && stream.pos == 2);
\end{code}

\texttt{next} runs a static regex at the current cursor. Failure returns
an empty optional and leaves \texttt{pos} unchanged. A successful
zero-length token raises an exception. Returned views refer to the
stream's storage; storage-changing operations can invalidate views in
C++. D slices retain their referenced allocation, but callers should
obtain fresh views after changing storage.

Unsigned
\texttt{read\_\allowbreak{}integer\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
/ \texttt{read\_\allowbreak{}integer!T} and
\texttt{write\_\allowbreak{}integer(\allowbreak{}value,\allowbreak{} order)\allowbreak{}}
use
\texttt{ByteOrder:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Little/\allowbreak{}Big} in
C++ or \texttt{ByteOrder.\allowbreak{}Little/\allowbreak{}Big} in D. The
default is little-endian; each operation consumes/appends the type's
byte width.

\texttt{BinaryStream.\allowbreak{}load(\allowbreak{}io,\allowbreak{} limit)\allowbreak{}}
repeatedly reads through abstract I/O until EOF. Its default limit is 64
MiB. Exceeding the configured limit raises an exception.
\texttt{save(\allowbreak{}io)\allowbreak{}} writes the whole buffer
through \texttt{write\_\allowbreak{}all}, regardless of \texttt{pos}.
The user codec can compose these operations into its own file/record
protocol.
